      "program_name": "Behavioral Health Services Act Integrated Plan FY 2026-29: housing interventions, capital development and operating subsidies",
      "administering_agency": "Fresno County Department of Behavioral Health (Integrated Plan approved by the Board June 16, 2026)",
      "funding_type": "other",
      "typical_award_size": "Housing Interventions component expenditures: $26,929,779 (FY 2026-27), $30,078,356 (FY 2027-28), $31,885,245 (FY 2028-29). Capital development $6,098,789 to $6,118,094 a year (24.8% of the component in year one, under the 25% cap), permanent rental subsidies $7,092,044 plus bundled rental and operating $2,526,608, interim rental subsidies $1,207,577 and bundled $2,250,773, outreach $1,709,758, participant assistance $373,266, Housing Flex Pool start-up $400,000 a year, flex pool share rising from 50% to 80%. First capital commitment: Victory Village (959 N. Parkway Drive, Fresno; Fresno Housing conversion of a shelter to 42 units, 41 PSH for chronically homeless) conditional $6,300,000 capital plus $287,000 a year operating subsidy for 30 years ($8,610,000) and on-site services valued at $424,872 a year, contingent on 2026 Round 2 9% tax credits (resolution June 30, 2026).",
      "per_unit_cap": "Victory Village: about $153,659 of BHSA capital per PSH unit; BHSA capital is limited to 25% of the Housing Interventions component.",
      "affordability_requirement": "BHSA eligible adults with serious mental illness or substance use disorder; at least 50% of housing funds for chronically homeless people; Victory Village carries a 30-year use restriction for 41 PSH units.",
      "eligible_project_types": [
        "supportive housing",
        "interim housing"
      ],
      "application_cycle": "rolling",
      "next_deadline": "No NOFA. Behavioral Health plans one BHSA capital project of about 42 units per plan period and will issue an RFP for a Housing Flex Pool operator; Behavioral Health Bridge Housing operating subsidies for 180 units at two sites convert to BHSA when BHBH ends in 2027. Developers should approach Behavioral Health housing staff with a PSH project that needs capital or a 20 to 30 year operating subsidy.",
      "prevailing_wage_trigger": "unclear",
      "layering_notes": "Fresno County splits housing work three ways: the Department of Public Works and Planning, Community Development Division (ComDev) runs the Urban County HUD programs, PLHA and the annual Affordable Housing Development NOFO; the County Administrative Office's new Office of Housing and Homelessness (OHH, created November 18, 2025) administers HHAP, the Continuum of Care and Transitional Rent and is absorbing housing programs from Behavioral Health and ComDev (Phase 2 approved November 3, 2026); and the Department of Behavioral Health co-applies for Homekey+ and NPLH and funds BHSA housing. Fresno Housing (the joint City and County Housing Authorities) is the dominant developer and voucher source. The City of Fresno and Clovis are separate entitlements with their own programs (Pass 3). fresnocountyca.gov and fresnohousing.org open normally; Board records are on fresnocounty.legistar.com and the Legistar web API client 'fresnocounty'. Fresno is one of the few counties in this database putting BHSA into capital (about $6.1 million a year) and into long operating subsidies ($287,000 a year for 30 years at Victory Village, $15.3 million over 20 years at Sendero Commons). Tenant-based subsidies serve 869 people in year one rising to 1,049, 372 units a year. Capital money has gone first to Fresno Housing (Victory Village) and to the Homekey+ project in Sanger. Source is the revised 2026-2029 Integrated Plan PDF; budget figures from the Integrated Plan Budget attachment to Board file 26-0538 and the Victory Village resolution (file 26-0585).",
